<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lop Nur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lop Nor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lop Nur is a former salt lake, now largely dry, in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The ancient oasis kingdom of Kroraina, called Loulan in Chinese, lay on its north-western shore, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between China and the western oases. Changes in the course of the Tarim River shifted and shrank the lake. From 1964 the area was China's nuclear test site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lop-nur.jpg" alt="Lop Nur" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA. Image courtesy of Earth Sciences and Image Analysis Laboratory, NASA Johnson Space Center. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basin_of_Lop_Nur_90.25E,_40.10N,_Desert_of_Lop,_Kum_Tagh_and_Astin_Tagh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lop Nur lies in the Lop Depression on the eastern fringe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, between the Taklamakan and Kumtag deserts. The name comes from an Oirat Mongolic name meaning Lop Lake, where Lop is a place name of unknown origin. The Tarim River and the Shule River drain into the lake system, which is the last remnant of the post-glacial Tarim Lake, once more than 10,000 square kilometres in area. The lake has no outlet and depended on meltwater from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tianshan</a>, Kunlun and western Qilian mountains. It measured 3,100 square kilometres in 1928, but dams on the rivers feeding it have dried it up, leaving a salt crust 30 to 100 centimetres thick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From about 1800 BCE until the 9th century the lake supported a Tocharian culture, and archaeologists have found buried settlements and several of the Tarim mummies along its old shore. The kingdom of Loulan, a civilisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, drew on the waters of the Tarim River and Lop Nur from the second century BCE. It became a client state of the Chinese empire in 55 BCE and was renamed Shanshan, and it was abandoned some time in the seventh century. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> crossed the Lop Desert between 395 and 414, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> later passed through it. Early Chinese texts called the lake Yan Ze, Salt Marsh, and held, mistakenly, that it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> by an underground channel.</p><p>The lake was known as the Wandering Lake in the early 20th century, because the Tarim River changed course and moved its terminal lake among the Lop Nur, Kara-Koshun and Taitema basins. Nikolay Przhevalsky found it at Kara-Koshun in 1867, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sven-hedin">Sven Hedin</a>, who visited in 1900 and 1901, suggested that such changes had caused Loulan to perish. Human intervention moved the lake back in 1921, but it dried out again by 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> established the Lop Nur Nuclear Test Base on 16 October 1959, with Soviet help in choosing the site, and the first Chinese nuclear bomb was tested there on 16 October 1964. A full-scale thermonuclear weapon followed on 17 June 1967. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Forty-five</a> tests were conducted before 29 July 1996, when China held its last and announced a moratorium the next day.</p><p>Archaeological sites near the lake include the Bronze Age Xiaohe Cemetery, where more than thirty well-preserved mummies have been excavated, and Qäwrighul, whose graves date mostly from 2100 to 1500 BCE. At Miran, to the south-west, excavated Buddhist monasteries have murals and sculptures that show influences from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. A reserve protects the wild Bactrian camel, about half of whose remaining population lives on the former test base. Potash has been mined at the lake since the mid-1990s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lop Nur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China defeated India in a brief border war in 1962, tested its first atomic bomb at <b>Lop Nur</b> on 16 October 1964 and its first hydrogen bomb in 1967, and launched its first satellite in 1970.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kroraina, called Loulan in Chinese, was an oasis state on the north-western shore of <b>Lop Nur</b> in the eastern Tarim Basin, the first stop for travellers leaving China westward.</blockquote><a href="/state/kroraina">Kroraina</a></li></ul></section>
